| 187 | a `NOT` predicate inside a group under a repetition (`{ NOT ⦈ _ }*`) does not bound the repetition: the generated parser runs to the end of the file and the expander never closes | NEGATIVE-VERIFIED | implementation gap (parser) | silent | none | apl rung 1 | `Error occurred while instantiating and executing a temporary parser: … Syntax Error`, one line past the end of the use site. Sites in `ProjectFortress/src/com/sun/fortress/parser/Syntax.rats`: `:249-265` (`NOT`), `:281-287` (`*`), `:325-326` (groups). Workaround: a right-recursive nonterminal that applies the predicate one character at a time, `NOT ⦈ _ t:AplCmt \| NOT ⦈ _`; it lets `apl⦇ ⍬≡⍴5 ⍝ Does zilde match shape of 5? ⦈` accept the book's comment. Verified on the retired first library, in a worktree of `0ff5eae27~1`; probe `explorations/apl/v1-probes/p09_comment.fss` (works; `.out.0` the failure), in the parent of 1ee3b0bc5. full text: history, row 187 |
| 202 | a name can be **read** through an `Id` gap only as a call argument, not as the whole template, parenthesised or juxtaposed, so a sub-language's reference must be one grammar alternative per name, a closed name set | NEGATIVE-VERIFIED | implementation gap (parser) | `Specification/advanced/domain-specific-languages.tex`, "Support for Domain-Specific Languages" | none | apl rung 2b | As the whole template, `Parameter 'text' to the IdOrOp constructor was null` at `TemplateParser4.pExpression$AssignLeft`. Juxtaposed (`<[ q03ref n ]>`), `Parameter 'apiName' to the IdOrOpOrAnonymousName constructor was null`: `Transform.forVarRef` (`Transform.java:122-124`) looks a `VarRef`'s id up in the hygiene environment, never substituting a gap inside it. Parenthesised (row 182, its duplicate): `MacroError: Could not parse '( <!@#$%^&*<Id i >*&^%$#@!> ) + 1 '`; workaround `⍎(v)`. The paper's Fig. 5 substitutes into any term position. Probes `explorations/apl/v1-probes/q02b_g.fsi`, `explorations/apl/v1-probes/q03_g.fsi`, in the parent of 1ee3b0bc5. full text: history, row 202 |
| 203 | a terminal that is a **valid identifier becomes a keyword of the whole language**, so writing the name `v` as a terminal to recognise it makes `v` unreadable as a name everywhere, templates included | NEGATIVE-VERIFIED | implementation gap (parser) | silent | none | apl rung 2b | `ItemDisambiguator.java:109-122` makes it a `KeywordSymbol`, and `ParserMaker.java:296-316` adds every keyword of a user grammar to `FORTRESS_KEYWORDS`, after which `Id` excludes it: `MacroError: Could not parse '(v) '`. The template `<[ (v) ]>` alone parses (`q05`), so the production poisons it. A bare `apl` terminal likewise reserves `apl`; `apl⦇` is no identifier, so a `TokenSymbol`. Workaround, verified: a character class, `[v]`, a `CharacterClassSymbol` that reserves nothing. The paper is silent: a terminal is "any sequence of Unicode characters" (§4). Probes `explorations/apl/v1-probes/q04_g.fsi` and the two after it, q05 and q06, in the parent of 1ee3b0bc5. full text: history, row 203 |
| 207 | on the lambda route a name can be bound **once per block**, and not if the host already has it: a second `←` on the same name, and a host local of the binder's name, are each `Variable v is already declared` | NEGATIVE-VERIFIED | design limit (specification) | `Specification/basic/declarations.tex`, "Reach and Scope of Declarations" | none | apl rung 2b | A limit of the host's scoping, not of the macro system. A second `←` is a nested lambda parameter of that name (at 16:62 of `explorations/apl/v1-probes/q07_rebind.fss`); a host local of the binder's name collides at the use site (at 14:38 of `explorations/apl/v1-probes/q01_bind.fss.0`, output `q01_bind.out.0`). Every indexed assignment (`v[3] ← ¯1`) is a rebinding, `AplArr` being a `value object`. Workaround: the lambda binds a **cell**, so `←` after the first is a `put` and the chapter's assignments all run (Ex 5, 37, 38). The paper is silent: hygiene renames a macro's own binders, and pattern variables are never renamed. Both probes in the parent of 1ee3b0bc5. full text: history, row 207 |
| 210 | a sub-language phrase with **no terminator** silently runs onto the next line whenever that line can extend it, because the optional whitespace `w` that a space between symbols becomes crosses line breaks | NEGATIVE-VERIFIED | design limit (parser) | silent | none | apl rung 2b | siblings: 244. `ProjectFortress/src/com/sun/fortress/parser/Spacing.rats:92` `w = Whitespace*`, which `ComposingSyntaxDefTranslator.java:147-148` emits for a space. `aply 1 2` above `3 4` prints `1 2 3 4` (`explorations/apl/v1-probes/q11_nlform.fss`, in the parent of 1ee3b0bc5). In `apl⦇ ⦈` a next line extends a statement if it starts with a numeral (row 228), a dyadic glyph (238), a dfn's operand (241), or a name under a strand rule over names (253). What separates: `⋄`; a trailing `⍝` comment (row 227); a break above a glyph with no dyadic rule (`⍉`); row 209's `AND NEWLINE`. A regex is safe: a slash closes it. full text: history, row 210 |
| 242 | **`_` is not accepted by an `Id` gap**: `_ ← e`, APL's throw-away name, is a Syntax Error where the same line with an ordinary name runs | NEGATIVE-VERIFIED | implementation gap (parser) | `Specification/basic/expressions/blocks.tex`, "Do Expressions" | none | apl rung 4 | `u18_gram.fss:27:34: Syntax Error` for `{a ← 3 3⍴1 ⋄ _ ← {a[1;1] ← 0}⍬ ⋄ a} 0` (`explorations/apl/rung-4/u18_gram.out.1`, in the parent of 1ee3b0bc5); the tracked `u18_gram.fss` has `x` in place of `_`. The reported column is three tokens early: the furthest-failure position. By reading, not run: `Id` (`ProjectFortress/src/com/sun/fortress/parser/Identifier.rats:52-54`) has no `_`, which only `BindId` (`:62-65`) adds. `_` binds in the language proper (`_ = e`), and walk takes it as a lambda parameter (`explorations/apl/rung-4/u01_name.fss`). Workaround: an ordinary name, `x ← {a[1;1] ← 0}⍬` (Ex 23). full text: history, row 242 |
| 244 | a **line break inside a production's symbol sequence is not optional whitespace**: it is a required line break at the use site, so a rule split across two lines never matches a use written on one | NEGATIVE-VERIFIED | design limit (parser) | silent | none | apl rung 4 | siblings: 210, 208. `ComposingSyntaxDefTranslator.java:147-149` maps `SPACE` and a literal space to `w`; a newline is not one of them and becomes `br` (`:152-153`). Seen with `(a,b,c)⍴x` split between `)` and `SPACE ⍴`, and with the `MyFirstFunction` alternative of `AplFnName` with its `NOT [A:Za:z0:9]` on the next line (`explorations/apl/rung-4/u17_gram.out.0`, `.out.1`, `.out.4`, `Syntax Error` at 51:77 and 70:39 of the failing forms, in the parent of 1ee3b0bc5); both match on one line, as the tracked `u17_gram.fss` has them. Workaround: a long production on one line. The break before `=>` is free: every rule in `explorations/apl/base/AplSyntax.fsi` takes it. full text: history, row 244 |
| 269 | a `grammar` declaration is accepted only in an api, never in a component; the frozen specification's component-level syntax declaration, `ExternalSyntax` (`syntax … = Expr`), is a different form, unimplemented (row 267) | NEGATIVE-VERIFIED | design limit (parser) | `Specification-1.0-frozen/basic/declarations.tex:113-118`, "Kinds of Declarations" | none | apl feasibility | siblings: 267. `ProjectFortress/src/com/sun/fortress/parser/Declaration.rats:42-51` (`Decl`) has no `GrammarDef`; `AbsDecl` (`:70-92`) has it at `:92`. The failure is the preparser's, not a named diagnostic: `Error occurred while instantiating and executing a temporary parser: … (…:8:8: Syntax Error)`. Workaround: ship a sub-language as three files, a grammar api, a stub component exporting it, and the using component, as `explorations/apl/base/AplSyntax.fsi` and `AplCore.fss` do; row 190 adds that the three need not sit in one directory. Probe `explorations/apl-probes/b11_gram_in_component.fss`, in the parent of 7c3218956. full text: history, row 269 |
| 273 | a `*`-bound gap used without the `**` splice crashes the generated parser with a raw Java cast error and no Fortress diagnostic | NEGATIVE-VERIFIED | implementation gap (parser) | silent | none | apl feasibility | siblings: 274. `Error occurred while instantiating and executing a temporary parser: com.sun.fortress.parser.templateparser.TemplateParser15 (class java.util.LinkedList cannot be cast to class com.sun.fortress.nodes.Node …)`. `ComposingSyntaxDefTranslator.java:489-490`, `forListDepth`, returns the raw Java list (`return source;`) with its conversion commented out beneath (`:491-504`), so the generated parser hands a `LinkedList` where a `Node` is expected. Workaround: a repeated gap's one legal use, the typed splice of row 272. Probe `explorations/apl-probes/b06b_repeat.fss`, in the parent of 7c3218956. full text: history, row 273 |
| 274 | `?` works on an unbound grammar symbol, but a bound `?` pattern variable (a `Maybe`-typed gap) is unimplemented: `MacroError: not supported now` | NEGATIVE-VERIFIED | implementation gap (parser) | silent | none | apl feasibility | siblings: 273. The unbound form prints `7` with and without the optional symbol; the bound one is `Exception in thread "main" com.sun.fortress.exceptions.MacroError: not supported now`, thrown at `ComposingSyntaxDefTranslator.java:508-509`, `forOptionDepth`, with eleven lines of implementation commented out beneath (`:510-522`). Rows 180 and 197 lean on it: APL's reduce slash cannot be made optional inside one production, and an elided axis (`m[1;]`) needs a production of its own rather than a `Maybe` gap. Probes `explorations/apl-probes/b07a_option.fss` (works) and `explorations/apl-probes/b07b_optvar.fss` (fails), in the parent of 7c3218956. full text: history, row 274 |
